Aertun NEWS
***********

Unreleased
==========

* The Aertun Evidence Protocol, version 1: every party that sees an AI
  agent's action can say so in a signed receipt, and a reconciliation of
  those receipts says whether the parties agree.  Receipts are SCITT signed
  statements (RFC 9943): COSE_Sign1 with CWT claims, signed with Ed25519
  (RFC 9864, algorithm -19) or ML-DSA-65 (RFC 9964, -49), with the key's
  RFC 9679 thumbprint as its kid.  The action digest is SHA-256 over the
  resource, the tool and the RFC 8785 digest of the arguments, so the
  agent, the gateway and the server compute it alike.  `aertun receipt
  emit` makes one; `aertun receipt register` enters it in this
  deployment's transparency log, which answers with an RFC 9942 receipt in
  each algorithm, and verification requires both; `aertun receipt
  reconcile` names every action an expected party never reported, every
  gap or repeat in a party's sequence numbers and every action the parties
  name different actors for, and signs the result; `aertun receipt verify`
  checks any of them offline, and checks a reconciliation by doing it
  again.  Once a receipt log is set up, the agent interface appends a
  receipt from the target's vantage for every tool call it answers, on
  HTTP and on stdio (with admin.base_url set), and `aertun receipt export`
  writes the log's entries with their receipts on demand: an agent's own
  receipts reconcile against the server's with no change to the agent.

* The protocol is written down in spec/aep/v1, with its CDDL and fifteen
  test vectors: valid receipts in each algorithm, a transparent statement,
  agreeing and disagreeing reconciliations, and nine that a verifier must
  refuse, each for its own reason.  Both algorithms sign
  deterministically, and the suite requires the vectors to be reproduced
  byte for byte.  `aertun conform` runs them against this verifier, or
  against any other given as a command.  STABILITY.md says what never
  changes (anything signed), and THREAT_MODEL.md what is and is not
  defended.  Statements and both receipts were verified by an independent
  implementation (a separate CBOR reader, Python's Ed25519 and OpenSSL's
  ML-DSA-65).

* Fixed: a scheduled collection whose read succeeded and whose store then
  failed (a full disk, the event store refusing) lost those records for
  good.  The connector's checkpoint was saved before the records were
  stored, and their digests were remembered as seen, so the next read
  started after them and would have skipped them anyway.  The checkpoint
  is now saved only once every record is stored, and exactly the records
  written are remembered: the next read asks again for the rest, and a
  store that wrote its lines and then failed to save its index stores
  nothing twice.  Present since 0.1.0.

* Fixed: the scheduled estate sync, which reads every endpoint of a tool,
  moved the checkpoint of its event endpoints (Okta's system log, for one)
  without storing their events, so scheduled collection then started after
  events nobody stored.  It now moves only the checkpoints of what the
  estate keeps.  Checkpoints are also merged into the file as it is when
  saved, so one reader no longer undoes another's.  Present since 0.1.0.

* Fixed: a pushed delivery (a webhook, an Okta event hook, a shared signal,
  another site's audit stream) whose store failed after the sender had
  been told it was received was dropped.  It now waits in the queue and is
  tried again on every pass and at least every thirty seconds, with what
  was already written counted as stored and only the rest tried again;
  nothing of it is counted, remembered or signalled until it is stored,
  and its automation signals are raised once.  A delivery that fails for
  an hour, or is still waiting when the server stops, is given up on with
  an inbound.dropped record in the audit log, never silently.  Present
  since 0.1.0.

* `aertun hook claude-code`: Claude Code under this deployment's kill
  switch, with a receipt for everything it does.  Run as its PreToolUse,
  PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure hook, it refuses every tool call of an
  agent that is disabled, unregistered, or whose registry cannot be read
  (exit 2, the one status that stops a call), and after each call signs an
  AEP receipt from the agent's vantage, in the agent's own sequence, with
  the outcome.  An MCP tool is receipted under the server's resource and
  its own tool name (--mcp SERVER=URL), so it reconciles against the
  server's receipt; the test suite reconciles one against Aertun's own
  agent interface.  `aertun receipt init` sets up the receipt log.

* Dead letters.  A record a source's mapping turns away (a required field
  absent, a time it cannot read, an event that would not be usable) is no
  longer only counted: it is kept, whole, because a mapping usually breaks
  when the source renames a field and the fixed mapping needs the new one.
  Values the secret detector recognises are masked first; a record over
  64 KiB is counted and not kept; each source keeps at most 1,000 for 14
  days, in files only the store's owner can read.  `aertun telemetry
  dead-letter` lists them with the paths they lacked, `replay SOURCE`
  maps them again with the current mapping and stores what now maps, and
  `purge SOURCE` removes them; the daily counts outlive the records, and
  the source-quality ledger now degrades a day on which the mapping turned
  away one record in twenty or more.

* A source-quality ledger.  For each source and each day (by arrival, so
  a finished day never changes and is measured once), it counts events,
  duplicates, lateness, events stamped ahead of their arrival, backfills,
  and which fields events carried, and judges the day against the
  source's own previous fourteen: silent, under half its usual volume,
  a field nearly every earlier event carried gone from most of today's,
  four times later than usual, clocks ahead, or one event in twenty
  repeated is degraded; a spike or a backfill is a notice; a day as quiet
  as the same weekday last week is quiet by habit.  A day is a witness,
  its silence evidence that nothing happened, only when it is judged, not
  degraded, and not sampled: the OTLP receiver now keeps OpenTelemetry's
  sampling threshold from a span's tracestate (and nothing else from it),
  and a source that drops spans by design is a notice every day and never
  a witness.  `aertun telemetry quality` shows it; the server records each newly degraded day in the
  audit log, and the new rule aertun.source-quality-degraded (ATT&CK
  T1562.006) opens a finding on it.

* `aertun backup` writes the whole store, keys and secrets included, into
  one file encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a passphrase (PBKDF2-SHA-256,
  600,000 iterations), in authenticated chunks that cannot be reordered or
  cut short unnoticed.  The server need not stop: the audit and receipt
  logs are measured under their locks and copied as whole prefixes.
  `aertun restore` goes into a new directory only, refuses names that
  would escape it and anything but files and directories, holds every file
  to the manifest, and then holds the audit log to the head recorded at
  backup, to every head it signed and to every root it anchored.  The test
  suite runs the restore drill on every change.

* `aertun serve` answers /metrics in the Prometheus text format: HTTP
  responses by status class, agent-interface calls by outcome and the
  receipts kept for them, scheduled job runs and when each last
  succeeded, readiness, whether the audit log is taking records, the
  receipt log's size, and the Go runtime.  It answers a bearer token
  presented with the request (never one from the server's own
  environment), refuses agents' tokens and key-bound ones, and names no
  person, agent or tool.  /healthz and /readyz are unchanged.

* Sigma global filters (the meta filters of specification 2.1.0) are read
  and applied: each filter's condition is added as "and not" to the rules
  it names, where the log source matches exactly, and `rules: any` tunes
  every rule with that log source.  Filters may carry `expires`, `owner`,
  `ticket` and `approved_by`, which other Sigma tools ignore; a filter past
  its expiry is not applied and is named, and one with no expiry is counted
  as never expiring.  A file of filters used to be refused as malformed
  rules.  In `windtunnel run --sigma`, a library whose filters tuned
  anything is replayed again without them, and each filter is named beside
  the attacks it hides and the benign false positives it removes.

* Incidents involving AI systems have their own clocks.  Five decisions
  start them — ai-serious, ai-critical, ai-death, ai-breach, ai-imminent —
  under four new regimes: `aiact` (EU AI Act Article 73: fifteen days, two
  for critical infrastructure or a widespread infringement, ten for a
  death), `gpai` (the General-Purpose AI Code of Practice: two, five, ten
  and fifteen days by severity), `sb53` (California: fifteen days to the
  Office of Emergency Services, twenty-four hours where death or serious
  injury is imminent) and `raise` (New York, seventy-two hours, from
  1 January 2027).  `aertun incident prove` signs them like any other
  clock.

* Your estate's own weird-but-benign workflows, measured.  `aertun benign
  capture` selects a real session from the event store, refuses it when the
  intelligence store, an open finding or an unacknowledged alert says it is
  not benign, keeps only the fields the rules read, pseudonymises
  identifiers with a keyed HMAC that keeps each shape (an address stays in
  its special-purpose range and keeps its shared prefixes), masks secrets and
  personal data in free text, and refuses when sanitising changed what a rule
  does.  A second administrator approves it; it expires; it can be revoked.
  `aertun benign bundle` seals approved sessions into a dual-signed,
  content-addressed file, and `aertun benign verify` checks one offline.

* `aertun windtunnel run --perturb all` (or a list of dup, reorder, skew,
  burst, drop-field, observed-time, truncate) replays each scenario's session and each benign session
  again under the faults real pipelines put into telemetry: a quarter of
  events delivered twice, extra arrival delay up to twice the stream's own
  lateness, the busiest source's clock five minutes ahead, the session four
  times faster, the field the rules read most removed, the event's own time
  lost and its arrival time used (`observed-time`), and raw values cut at
  1,024 bytes and messages at 2,048 (`truncate`).  Per fault it
  reports the attacks missed, the catches that broke, and the benign
  sessions that started firing, with Wilson bounds.

* `aertun windtunnel run --baseline BUNDLE --baseline-key F` replays each
  benign session and reports the false positives the rules raise on it, per
  session, per rule and per class, with Wilson bounds, and respells the
  sessions under `--mutate`.  A rule that reads a dropped field, or compares
  a pseudonymised one with a literal and was not in the session's fidelity
  check, is named as not measurable, never counted as quiet.  Expired
  sessions are left out and named.  `windtunnel diff` fails on every rule
  newly firing on a benign session unless the pair is accepted with a reason
  and an expiry (`--accept FILE`, at most 180 days).


Version 0.2.0
=============

Aertun 0.2 holds more of security work to evidence: detection coverage
technique by technique, each dependency against the release before it, AI-written
vulnerability reports against the source they cite, managed detection tested
blind, alerts sealed by a Bitcoin block, a protocol's on-chain posture, a VEX
issuer's record and another site's audit log, each as a signed proof, and any
proven one as an in-toto verification result a policy engine reads.  It meets
the tools already in place: Wazuh in both directions, LiteLLM's gateway
records and traces, a GitHub Action, Kubernetes manifests and a Helm chart.

Upgrading from 0.1:

* A feed for a signed audit stream is now the kind `stream`; one added under
  the earlier name must be removed and added again (below).
* azure/activity keeps `raw.operation`, `raw.status` and `raw.category`
  where it kept one `raw.value`, and duo/auth `raw.user_name` and
  `raw.application` where it kept one `raw.name`: each overwrote another.
  A rule of your own reading the old names needs the new ones.
* The detection proof's verifier is version 2: an entry labelled as an attack
  is no longer counted as one a rule must stay quiet on.

* Wazuh, both ways.  `aertun inbound add webhook wazuh --mapping
  wazuh/alerts --seal` takes a manager's alerts, signed as Standard Webhooks
  deliveries by `integrations/wazuh/custom-aertun.py`, which Wazuh's
  integrator runs for each alert.  `--seal`, on any webhook feed, commits
  each delivered record's digest to the audit log in the feed's name, in
  one entry for each feed's batch within 30 seconds of arrival (at once for
  a full batch), so
  `aertun proof sealed --alert FILE` later finds the alert as its sender
  wrote it.  A mapping installed here before 0.2 for wazuh/alerts stays in
  place of the shipped one.  The new `wazuh/alerts` mapping reads alerts as a 4.14 manager
  writes them.  `integrations/wazuh/ruleset/rules/aertun_rules.xml` (IDs
  998000-998017) raises Wazuh alerts from Aertun's audit log.  The rule on a
  seal is level 0, so an alert is never sent back to be sealed again.  All
  of it was tested against a live 4.14.10 manager; the README says what was
  and was not.

* A mapping's `keep` may name the field a path is kept under ("rule.mitre.id
  as mitre"), and keeps a list of plain values joined by spaces.  Two kept
  paths ending alike are now refused when a mapping is added; one installed
  before still loads.  That found two shipped mappings
  overwriting a field: azure/activity now keeps `raw.operation`,
  `raw.status` and `raw.category` where it kept one `raw.value`, and
  duo/auth keeps `raw.user_name` and `raw.application` where it kept one
  `raw.name`.

* `aertun vuln referee REPORT --source DIR --key F` holds a vulnerability
  report's static claims, usually a model's, to a checkout of the source it
  is about, and signs what came of it.  The report is JSON in the new
  format aertun/vuln-report/v1, read strictly.  It is refuted when a cited
  file is not there, a cited function is not defined in it (Go, JavaScript
  and TypeScript, Python, C and C++, Rust, Java and Kotlin, Ruby and PHP),
  or a line is past its end; when the source declares another version, or
  the version is outside the range the report gives, or the range is
  inverted; when its CVSS 3 vector scores otherwise or the score is not of
  the severity given; and when an earlier report of the same flaw was
  committed or refereed here.  It is inconclusive when the checkout is not
  at the report's commit, or a claim cannot be checked.  `--asset DIR`
  asks whether an asset's own Go code references the vulnerable symbol.
  Proofs of concept are never run.  `aertun vuln commit REPORT` writes the
  report's digest and its flaw's fingerprint to the audit log, and nothing
  else of it: a referee proof names that entry as the report's priority,
  and the moment it was known.  Paths and names the report chose are
  carried only as digests.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/referee/v1.

* `aertun vuln continuity BILL --key F` holds each npm, PyPI and Go package
  in a bill of materials (CycloneDX or SPDX) to the release before it, as
  its registry describes both, and signs what came of it.  A package is
  refuted when it lost what its predecessor had: provenance, trusted
  publishing, the repository, workflow or tag it was built from, its
  publishing account's email, or its having no install script (or the
  script changed); and when its registry has withdrawn it, its publisher
  yanked it, or the provenance served is about another artifact by digest.
  The predecessor is the greatest earlier version still served and
  published before it.  It is proven when every package was read and none
  lost anything, and inconclusive when one could not be read.  The axios
  releases of 31 March 2026 are refused as they were published (provenance
  and trusted publishing lost, and an install script added by the new
  dependency), and today because npm withdrew them.  Publishers'
  names, emails, repositories and workflows are carried only as sha256
  digests.  Registries are read through the new network purpose
  `registry`; `--npm`, `--pypi` and `--goproxy` name mirrors.  The
  predicate is https://aertun.github.io/proof/continuity/v1.

* `aertun proof coverage --key F` signs, for each technique the rules
  claim, whether the claim holds.  A corpus entry can now carry
  `"techniques"`: the ATT&CK, ATLAS or OWASP ids of what produced it, such
  as the Atomic Red Team test that was run, never a rule's own tag.  A
  technique is refuted when an entry labelled with it is caught by no rule
  claiming it, or when a rule claiming it that raises an alert fires on an
  entry labelled benign.  It is proven when every instance is caught on at
  least `--min-positives` instances and `--min-benign` benign entries, and
  inconclusive when an instance is seen only by a rule that raises nothing
  on its own, or the evidence is too thin.  A sub-technique is an instance
  of its parent and not the reverse.  With `--telemetry 7d` each instance
  caught is asked again with only the fields its source sent in that window,
  read from the store (which takes tune): one no longer caught keeps its
  technique from being proven, and a rule catching none of its own examples
  that way is listed as dark, with the fields its sources stopped sending.
  Techniques the corpus labels and no rule claims are listed.  There is no
  percentage anywhere.  `--navigator FILE` draws the result as an ATT&CK
  Navigator layer (format 4.5): each technique coloured by its verdict and
  saying it in words, with no score.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/coverage/v1.

* `detect replay` no longer counts a rule firing on an entry labelled with
  a technique as a false positive, and a detection proof no longer counts
  one as an entry the rule must stay quiet on.  The detection verifier's
  version is now 2.

* `aertun vendor check-breach FILE` holds a vendor's disclosure of its own
  breach — its window, the indicators it published and what it told you —
  against your own events, and signs a breach-scope proof.  An indicator
  seen before or after the window refutes the window; one seen inside it
  refutes "not affected"; each is named with how many events carried it.
  Proven needs the store to hold the whole of the window and a margin
  either side (30 days by default), so not finding an indicator means it
  was not there.  The disclosure's title is never read.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/breach-scope/v1.

* `aertun incident prove ID --trigger aware` signs when an incident's clock
  started and whether what it started was done in time.  The decision's
  time and every discharge's are taken from the verified audit log, and
  the incident's own record is held against them: a clock moved in it
  afterwards refutes, and so does an anchored log head the log no longer
  matches, an entry dated more than two hours after the block that anchors
  it, an obligation discharged late or one past due.  Proven needs a
  verified person's decision under a log head anchored in a Bitcoin block
  whose header was checked, and every obligation discharged or ruled out;
  until then it is inconclusive and says why.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/clock/v1.

* `aertun vuln vex-record --source NAME --since DATE --tolerance R` signs a
  VEX issuer's track record from the not-affected proofs kept here about
  it.  Each kept proof is verified again as it is read; one that no longer
  verifies is counted and never used.  Only proofs signed by a key this
  store trusts are used (`aertun proof trust`, which takes two
  administrators), unless `--include-untrusted` asks for the rest and the
  record says so: anybody who may hand a proof in can make a key that signs
  one.  The others are counted, and none can supersede a trusted proof.
  The same document about the same asset proved again counts once, from
  its latest proof.  The rate is contradicted statements over those
  supported or contradicted, with exact one-sided bounds; uncheckable ones
  are counted and never in it.  It is refuted when the lower bound is over
  the tolerance, naming the contradicting proofs by their ids in the store;
  proven when the upper bound is at or under it on at least 30 checked
  statements; inconclusive otherwise.  Reversals are not counted, because a
  not-affected proof does not name the statements it supported.  The
  predicate is https://aertun.github.io/proof/vex-record/v1.

* `aertun chain posture FILE --rpc URL` signs whether a protocol's
  on-chain posture is what it declares.  The posture file names the chain
  and, for each Safe multisig, the least threshold and number of owners;
  for each OpenZeppelin TimelockController, the least delay; for each
  EIP-1967 proxy, its admin and optionally its implementation.  Every
  setting is read at one block (the latest finalized unless `--block`
  names one), each read naming the block by its hash.  A setting weaker
  than declared refutes it, by contract, setting, declared and found; it
  is proven only when every setting was read and holds, and otherwise
  inconclusive, saying which could not be read and why.  The endpoint is
  reached over HTTPS through the new network purpose `chain`, a node
  inside the organisation only when `--reach` declares its range, and its
  URL is never written down.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/chain-posture/v1.  A rule,
  evm.timelock-delay-changed, raises a timelock's delay changing, beside
  the rules for a multisig's signers and a proxy's upgrade.

* `aertun alert seal FILE` commits alerts from any monitor — an MDR's,
  a web3 monitor's, Aertun's own findings exported — to the audit log by
  digest: one entry per batch carries the Merkle root over the SHA-256 of
  each alert's RFC 8785 canonical JSON, the digests are kept beside the
  store, and the alerts themselves are not kept.  A detection run seals
  the findings it raises in its own entry.  `aertun proof sealed --alert
  FILE` (or `--finding ID`) signs when one existed: proven when a Bitcoin
  attestation whose block was checked covers a head of the verified log
  that includes the entry that sealed it, so it existed no later than that
  block; inconclusive while the head is pending, its timestamp names a
  block nobody has checked, or nothing anchored covers it, saying the time
  rests on the log alone; refuted when the digest is
  in no sealed batch, its path to the batch's root or the entry's to the
  head does not verify, or the log does not — including a head sent to be
  anchored that the log no longer matches.  `aertun alert seal --list`
  lists the batches.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/sealed/v1.

* `aertun mdr plan NAME --provider P --emulations F --count N` tests a
  managed detection provider, or an AI SOC, blind on your own estate.  The
  plan draws emulations from your list at random times in a window (30
  days by default) and commits its digest to the audit log before any is
  shown; `aertun mdr next` says only what has fallen due, never when the
  next one comes.  A verified person records each run (`mdr ran`), which
  counts only within a day of falling due, and each notification
  (`mdr notified --item N --at TIME --ref REF`, or `--source S` to join
  the provider's alerts from the event store by host and time, never by
  guessing).  `aertun mdr prove NAME --sla 30m --tolerance R` signs the
  missed share — notified late or not at all — with exact one-sided
  bounds: refuted when the lower bound is over the tolerance, naming each
  missed item; proven when the upper bound is at or under it on at least
  30 runs; inconclusive otherwise, and always until the test has closed,
  so it cannot be stopped on a good day.  The emulations are named only
  for the items missed, and hosts never.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/mdr/v1.

* Running it where enterprises run things.  A GitHub Action
  (`uses: Aertun/Aertun@v0.2.0`) fetches the release for the runner,
  checks it against SHA256SUMS and its build attestation, and runs the
  command given, one argument a line, never written into a script; a
  proof command fails the job unless it is proven.  `deploy/kubernetes`
  and the Helm chart `deploy/helm/aertun` run one replica with its
  volume, as the image's nonroot user, with a read-only root filesystem,
  no capabilities, the default seccomp profile, the restricted Pod
  Security level and a NetworkPolicy; checked with helm lint --strict,
  kubeconform and the image run that way.  The console answers /healthz
  (serving) and /readyz (the audit log can take a record) with a word and
  nothing more.

* `aertun proof svr ID|FILE --key F` sums a proven proof up as an in-toto
  Simple Verification Result (https://in-toto.io/attestation/svr/v0.2),
  the predicate policy engines admit artifacts on: about the same
  artifacts as the proof, with one property a policy asks for —
  AERTUN_DETECTION_PROVEN, AERTUN_NOT_AFFECTED_PROVEN and so on — the
  proof's predicate as the verifier and policy, and the proof's digest
  and key on it so the evidence can be found.  Only a proven proof has
  one: a result lists what passed.  Its Ed25519 signature is plain DSSE,
  and its ML-DSA one is in a domain of its own, so a result is never read
  as a proof nor a proof as a result.

* `aertun agent reconcile NAME` holds an agent's own account against two
  more witnesses when they are declared: a model gateway's record of the
  agent's key (`--gateway litellm/requests:HASH`), whose every call must
  be in the account, and with `--gateway-only` every model call in the
  account must be at the gateway; and what the systems it acts on
  recorded of its identity there (`--identity github/audit:LOGIN`,
  repeatable), each action of which must be near something the agent did
  — by its account, at the gateway or at the agent interface.  An action
  with nothing of the agent near is its identity used outside it.  Tool
  names are matched across a host's and a gateway's ways of prefixing
  them (mcp__github__create_issue is github-create_issue).  What does not
  match is named by kind, source and time, never by a model's, a tool's
  or an action's name, which the caller chose.  A reconciliation without
  either reads, and verifies, as it did.

* The last administrator cannot take their own administration away, on a
  store with an Owner or without one: revoking it, denying it, narrowing
  it to one area, giving it an end date, from the command line, the
  console or an identity provider's provisioning, is refused while nobody
  else administers the whole store.  A store made before Owners could
  lock itself out from the command line; it no longer can.

* An administrator is not made while their link to add a passkey waits
  unused: whoever opens a link first adds theirs, so until the person has
  used it, approving the grant would make an administrator of whoever had
  the link.  The approval waits, on the command line and the People
  screen, and goes through once the link has been used or ended.  A
  second administrator is still set up in either order — the grant or
  the passkey first, or both at once with `aertun auth invite NAME
  admin` — and the grant now takes effect only after the passkey.

* A token is issued at most at its holder's role: `aertun token issue
  NAME --principal WHO --role R` refuses a role above what WHO holds now,
  naming the grant to make first.  A token's power was already the lesser
  of its role and its holder's when used; one labelled higher waited, and
  became that much stronger the day its holder was granted more, with
  nobody looking at it.  So the grant comes first and the token after.

* LiteLLM, and any model gateway, is read two ways.  Its logging
  callback (`generic_api`) posts every call to a new bearer feed, `aertun
  inbound add webhook litellm --mapping litellm/requests --bearer`: a
  static secret in Authorization, for senders that can set a header and
  cannot sign (also Alertmanager, falcosidekick, SIEM and SOAR webhook
  actions), as a JSON array or one object a line, the same body twice
  taken once.  The shipped litellm/requests mapping reads the call's key
  by its hash as the actor, its alias, team and owner, the model, cost,
  tokens, MCP tool and guardrail status, and never what was asked or
  answered.  Its OpenTelemetry spans arrive at /v1/traces, which now reads
  OTLP protobuf as well as JSON — most SDKs send nothing else — and a span
  with no agent id is the key the gateway authenticated (raw.via names the
  gateway) rather than the gateway itself.  The current GenAI content
  attributes (gen_ai.input.messages, gen_ai.output.messages,
  gen_ai.system_instructions) are measured and dropped like the older
  ones.  Checked against a running LiteLLM proxy; its header must be
  written with a space, not %20, which it does not decode.  A new time
  layout, epoch-s-frac, reads seconds with a fraction exactly.

* `aertun inbound add stream NAME --keys FILE --head-context C` receives
  another program's signed audit stream when it signs its tree heads in a
  context of its own and its keys file does not say which.  The head
  key's published id is a fingerprint over its keys and its context, so a
  feed added with the wrong context is now refused when it is added,
  saying so, rather than refusing every batch afterwards; and a head that
  does not verify names the context it was checked in.

* `aertun inbound prove NAME --key F [--at TIME]` signs whether another
  site's audit log, received on a stream feed, is complete and unforked up
  to a time.  A stream feed now keeps every tree head the sender signs
  with the batch it came in.  A head signed with the sender's own keys
  that is not the log already held — records it sent before, now under
  another root — is a fork: answered 409, kept as evidence with its
  signatures, written once to this deployment's audit log (where the new
  rule aertun.peer-log-forked reads it, from this log or a peer's), and
  nothing is taken from it.  The proof rebuilds the log from what was
  kept and holds every head to it: refuted when a head is not the records
  held, or the sender forked — even when the evidence was deleted from
  the feed afterwards, since the audit log still records it; proven when
  the last batch arrived at or after the time asked about and was smaller
  than a full batch, so it reached the end of the sender's log; and
  inconclusive otherwise, because a site with nothing to send is silent
  and so is one that stopped sending.  The Feeds screen and `inbound
  status` show each peer's chain head and any fork.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/peer-log/v1.

* A Bitcoin timestamp counts only with its block.  An OpenTimestamps
  attestation is a block height and nothing more, and anybody who can
  write the store could append one to back-date a head; so `aertun
  auditlog anchor upgrade` now fetches the header of the block each
  attestation names from public explorers (three by default, or the ones
  `--explorer URL` names, such as a node of your own), takes it only when
  two return the same header and none returns another, and keeps it only
  when its merkle root is the value the timestamp reaches.  The clock and
  sealed-alert proofs check that again offline, with the header's work —
  it must meet its own target at a difficulty of at least 50 trillion —
  and carry the header, the block's hash and the time it was mined, so
  whoever reads one can look the block up in any node.  A timestamp with
  no checked header is no anchor: `anchor status` shows it as unchecked,
  and a proof resting on it is inconclusive.  Stores anchored before this
  need `aertun auditlog anchor upgrade` run once.

* `aertun proof revocation` signs whether a revocation held.  For an agent
  disabled with the kill switch (`--agent NAME`) it reads this program's
  own verified audit log, the agent's own telemetry and any identity the
  agent is declared to act as elsewhere; for an identity revoked in another
  system (`--identity ISSUER:VALUE --at TIME`) it reads every collected
  source.  Any act after the grace refutes it, by source, identity and
  time; attempts a source refused are counted beside.  It is proven only
  when a source had seen the identity act before and every such source
  delivered events in every stretch of the period, so silence means nothing
  happened rather than nothing was listening; otherwise it is inconclusive
  and says why.  The predicate is
  https://aertun.github.io/proof/revocation/v1.

* Bills of materials in SPDX 2.2 and 2.3 JSON are read as CycloneDX ones
  are (`vuln import --bom`, `sca scan --bom`): the package the document
  describes is the root, each package's purl is what advisories match, and
  DEPENDS_ON and DEPENDENCY_OF are the graph.  This is the form GitHub's
  dependency graph, Syft and Trivy export.  The 0.1.0 README said SPDX was
  read before it was; now it is.  SPDX 3 and the tag-value form are
  refused by name.

* The feed that receives a signed audit stream is the kind `stream`
  (`aertun inbound add stream NAME --keys FILE`): it takes what
  `aertun siem stream` sends, from another Aertun deployment or any program
  that sends the same format, and stores it as stream/audit with each actor
  named by the feed.  The published keys may name the context the sender
  signs its tree heads in (`head_context`), and `aertun siem stream key
  --json` now states its own.  Six of the rules over Aertun's own audit
  log — an administrator granted or given a token, the audit log moved,
  emergency recovery, a privileged change made alone, sign-in failures —
  read a stream too, so one deployment raises them about another.  A feed
  added under the kind's earlier name must be removed and added again, and
  the rules that read only that sender's records are gone.

Version 0.1.0
=============

The first release of Aertun, the proving ground for AI security work: it
holds what AI defenders produce to evidence the AI did not write, says
proven, refuted or inconclusive, and signs that, in one binary with no
dependencies.

The proving ground
------------------

* A proof is an in-toto statement in a DSSE envelope, signed with Ed25519
  and ML-DSA-65 whatever its verdict.  `aertun proof verify` exits non-zero
  on anything but proven, and so does every command that makes one, which
  makes each a gate.  scripts/verifyproof/verify.py checks both signatures
  with the Python cryptography package and the openssl command, and shares
  no code with this program.

* Nine kinds of proof: a detection rule against a labelled corpus (`proof
  detection`); the AI analyst on the shipped corpus, asked several times
  with instructions planted in the text a log carries (`proof analyst`);
  the analyst's threshold, fitted on what verified people ruled (`analyst
  calibrate`); another vendor's AI verdicts against people's decisions
  (`verdict prove`); the same on a random sample judged blind (`adjudicate
  prove`); a vendor's claim registered before its data arrived (`claim
  test`); an agent's own account against the calls and connections seen
  (`agent reconcile`); a VEX document's not_affected statements against the
  bill of materials, advisories and reachability (`vuln prove-vex`); and
  every citation in an AI's investigation run again against the event
  store (`proof evidence`).

* Kept proofs are listed on the console's Proofs screen, by `aertun proof
  list` and by the agent interface, verified again on every read.  A proof
  counts as the store's own only when its key is a trusted signer, which
  takes two administrators (`aertun proof signers | trust | untrust`).

* The wind tunnel replays scripted attacks on AI agents through a recorded
  telemetry stream and scores whether your own Sigma rules caught them
  (`aertun windtunnel run --sigma DIR`), with respellings of each session
  (`--mutate N`).  Scored against the shipped pack it is self-graded and
  says so.

* The context twin (`aertun twin record`, `aertun mcp --twin FILE`) answers
  an agent from one recorded file, the same bytes every time; the forge
  (`aertun forge export`) writes what the analyst saw and answered and how
  a person ruled as signed episodes.

The evidence
------------

* Collecting: ten connectors with sealed credentials, twenty log sources
  mapped to one event shape, scheduled collection, files, and feeds other
  systems push to (SSF and CAEP security events, Okta event hooks, signed
  webhooks, GenAI agent telemetry over OTLP, another deployment's signed
  audit stream).  Every event is kept append-only before anything reads it.

* Detecting: rules with fixtures, imported Sigma, trial and live rings,
  suppressions that end, correlations, and indicators of compromise.  The
  Sigma reader agrees with PyYAML on every file in the SigmaHQ repository,
  and 3,076 of its 3,164 core rules compile; the rest are refused for a
  construct named as not implemented.

* Deciding: one queue of findings ranked by risk, with every decision kept;
  an AI analyst that suggests and records no decision itself; hunting
  through the stored events.

* Responding: cases with the clocks the law sets (GDPR, NIS2, DORA, the
  Cyber Resilience Act, SEC, CIRCIA, HIPAA), playbooks, acts on other
  platforms approved by a second person, automations, and breach notices.

* Exposure: vulnerabilities decided with SSVC and published as VEX,
  dependency and code-scanning results, workforce, device and vendor risk,
  and access reviews.

People, agents and the record
-----------------------------

* One Owner, whom nobody can remove; the roles viewer, analyst, responder
  and admin, and the jobs engineer, vuln-manager and auditor; no custom
  roles.  `aertun auth invite` grants a role and prints the enrolment link.

* Privileged changes take two administrators.  An only administrator's
  request needs a reason, waits an hour (a day to move the audit log), and
  is then approved with a passkey sign-in made in the last five minutes.

* Signing in: passkeys, OpenID Connect and SAML 2.0, with SCIM
  provisioning; two factors for everybody; lockout by place; sessions that
  end after an hour unused and fifteen minutes for administrators.

* Agents are registered before they act, hold credentials that can be
  bound to one run, propose and never approve, sign or keep, and each has
  a kill switch on the console and the command line.  The agent interface
  (MCP) is twelve operations behind three tools.

* Its own record: an append-only audit log with a hash chain, a Merkle
  tree, tree heads signed with Ed25519 and ML-DSA-65, OpenTimestamps
  anchoring, inclusion proofs, SP 800-53 evidence packs, and a stream to
  any SIEM that reads OCSF.

* Three faces that agree: the console, the command line and the agent
  interface, from one set of declared operations.
